WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.411 --> 00:00:09.680
Equality is one of the most influential
and powerful words

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:12.441
in the development 
of what our society is today.

00:00:12.441 --> 00:00:15.162
It is a word that represents fairness.

00:00:15.162 --> 00:00:17.422
A word that signifies justice.

00:00:17.422 --> 00:00:19.531
A concept that people have fought for

00:00:19.531 --> 00:00:23.692
and that has revolutionized 
our global culture in innumerable ways.

00:00:23.692 --> 00:00:27.490
Today, I challenge you to consider
a different side of equality,

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:29.612
one that isn't always positive.

00:00:29.612 --> 00:00:35.082
My question today is can this amazing
concept of equality have another side?

00:00:35.082 --> 00:00:38.693
Can equality ever be unfair?

00:00:38.693 --> 00:00:41.702
If you were to look 
at America about 150 years ago,

00:00:41.702 --> 00:00:43.913
you would find that it was
radically different

00:00:43.913 --> 00:00:45.532
from the America we know today.

00:00:45.532 --> 00:00:48.493
For example, this was a time where
women were expected to wear

00:00:48.493 --> 00:00:51.312
a large hoop skirt 
and a very tight corset.

00:00:51.312 --> 00:00:55.364
A time when lower-class women were
expected to have a labor-intensive job

00:00:55.364 --> 00:01:00.764
such as a servant, a factory worker,
domestic help, etc.

00:01:00.764 --> 00:01:02.743
Middle and upper-class women were
expected

00:01:02.743 --> 00:01:04.803
to either help with the family business

00:01:04.803 --> 00:01:07.111
or to become a homemaker.

00:01:07.111 --> 00:01:12.091
This idea of equality inspired both women
and men alike to start a revolution

00:01:12.091 --> 00:01:16.273
that would come to completely
change the face of society for the better.

00:01:16.273 --> 00:01:18.645
Let's take a look at Mother Teresa.

00:01:18.645 --> 00:01:20.643
Born August 26, 1910,

00:01:20.643 --> 00:01:24.994
Saint Teresa of Calcutta was accepted
into the Loreto Convent in Dublin, Ireland

00:01:24.994 --> 00:01:26.636
at the age of 18.

00:01:26.636 --> 00:01:28.644
From there, she was sent to India.

00:01:28.644 --> 00:01:32.525
Although she was not an American,
in 1999, she was voted by Americans

00:01:32.525 --> 00:01:36.365
as "The Most Admired Person 
of the 20th Century."

00:01:36.365 --> 00:01:39.474
Her goal in life was to provide help
to the helpless,

00:01:39.474 --> 00:01:41.155
home to the homeless,

00:01:41.155 --> 00:01:43.396
love to the lonely.

00:01:43.396 --> 00:01:46.276
In her words, "Let us always meet
each other with a smile,

00:01:46.276 --> 00:01:50.005
for a smile is the beginning of love."

00:01:50.005 --> 00:01:52.905
Through her work, she advocated
for the rights of the people

00:01:52.905 --> 00:01:55.774
whom society had deemed unworthy.

00:01:55.774 --> 00:01:58.745
She dedicated herself entirely to her goal

00:01:58.745 --> 00:02:04.306
and by doing so, became one of the most
respected and beloved people ever to live.

00:02:04.306 --> 00:02:06.706
And she was a woman.

00:02:06.706 --> 00:02:08.617
Amelia Earhart.

00:02:08.617 --> 00:02:12.887
She was the first female aviator to ever
fly solo over the Atlantic Ocean.

00:02:12.887 --> 00:02:15.467
Did you know that Earhart was so admired
by the public

00:02:15.467 --> 00:02:16.805
that people would write her

00:02:16.805 --> 00:02:18.858
and tell her that they would name things,

00:02:18.858 --> 00:02:24.526
such as children, lakes, and even
sometimes homing pigeons Amelia after her?

00:02:24.526 --> 00:02:29.208
Earhart once said, "women must try
to do things as men have tried.

00:02:29.208 --> 00:02:33.197
And if they fail, their failure 
must be a challenge to others."

00:02:33.197 --> 00:02:37.338
These amazing women are just two examples
of the many people throughout history

00:02:37.338 --> 00:02:39.317
who not only broke stereotypes,

00:02:39.317 --> 00:02:40.897
but demolished them.

00:02:40.897 --> 00:02:44.799
They completely destroyed the idea
that women weren't strong enough

00:02:44.799 --> 00:02:47.978
or qualified enough to change the world.

00:02:47.978 --> 00:02:51.598
Now women's rights is no doubt
one of the first things to come to mind

00:02:51.598 --> 00:02:53.178
when thinking of equality.

00:02:53.178 --> 00:02:56.459
It rightfully gives us a sense of pride
and accomplishment.

00:02:56.459 --> 00:03:02.143
This brings us back to the question
of how can equality possibly be unfair?

00:03:02.143 --> 00:03:05.948
Let's take a moment to compare the word
Equality with the word Equity.

00:03:05.948 --> 00:03:09.923
As you can see, both words have
the same prefix that means equal.

00:03:09.923 --> 00:03:13.333
And at first, you may notice the only
difference between these words

00:03:13.333 --> 00:03:17.440
is two letters, "a" and "l".

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:22.429
I'm here today to tell you that
those two letters make all the difference.

00:03:22.429 --> 00:03:24.956
If we break through the layer
that is the "a" and the "l"

00:03:24.956 --> 00:03:28.242
we realize that equality and equity
have about as much in common

00:03:28.242 --> 00:03:30.530
as left and right.

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:32.992
Now, don't get me wrong.

00:03:32.992 --> 00:03:36.450
I'm not saying that equality
is a bad thing,

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:42.171
rather that it's not the best option
for every situation, such as this one.

00:03:42.171 --> 00:03:45.761
Personally, this is one of the most
powerful images I've ever seen.

00:03:45.761 --> 00:03:50.080
It beautifully illustrates a concept
so hard to explain.

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:53.944
As we can see, 
equality represents sameness,

00:03:53.944 --> 00:03:56.941
which in some situations is a great thing.

00:03:56.941 --> 00:03:59.992
Equity, on the other hand, 
represents fairness,

00:03:59.992 --> 00:04:03.081
which in all situations is the best thing.

00:04:03.081 --> 00:04:07.652
When each child gets the same number
of boxes, not everyone can see.

00:04:07.652 --> 00:04:10.823
But when each child gets the amount
of boxes they need,

00:04:10.823 --> 00:04:14.291
all three children are able
to see over the fence.

00:04:14.291 --> 00:04:16.631
My father is a special education 
science teacher

00:04:16.631 --> 00:04:19.382
and has been for the last eleven years.

00:04:19.382 --> 00:04:21.863
He says that the very first
thing he has to do

00:04:21.863 --> 00:04:25.523
is determine what the students' current
levels of educational functioning are,

00:04:25.523 --> 00:04:28.922
and from that, he can modify their
assignments and their exams

00:04:28.922 --> 00:04:31.013
to fit their current needs.

00:04:31.013 --> 00:04:33.914
He says that he views his job
as leveling the playing field

00:04:33.914 --> 00:04:37.271
to set every student up for success.

00:04:37.271 --> 00:04:39.371
Leveling the playing field.

00:04:39.371 --> 00:04:40.603
In my perspective,

00:04:40.603 --> 00:04:43.459
to level the playing field means to give
every student

00:04:43.459 --> 00:04:46.853
the opportunities and tools
to compete as equals

00:04:46.853 --> 00:04:49.435
in the game of life.

00:04:49.435 --> 00:04:52.126
Now, this not only applies to special
education students,

00:04:52.126 --> 00:04:54.854
but to gifted students as well.

00:04:54.854 --> 00:05:00.105
Research shows that roughly 18-25%
of gifted people are underachievers.

00:05:00.105 --> 00:05:03.603
They simply quit trying because 
nothing they do

00:05:03.603 --> 00:05:06.996
leads to any measurable success
or satisfaction.

00:05:06.996 --> 00:05:10.274
And in my opinion, this is unacceptable.

00:05:10.274 --> 00:05:14.606
School is supposed to be a place
to learn and to grow.

00:05:14.606 --> 00:05:17.941
But not all students grow well
in the same conditions.

00:05:17.941 --> 00:05:20.475
Oddly enough, neither do plants.

00:05:20.475 --> 00:05:24.813
A cactus can store water for long
periods of time.

00:05:24.813 --> 00:05:29.576
It doesn't need much of any attention,
and it is very large and very strong.

00:05:29.576 --> 00:05:31.005
An orchid, on the other hand,

00:05:31.005 --> 00:05:34.566
is a small, delicate flower that needs
the precise temperature

00:05:34.566 --> 00:05:37.116
and amount of water in order to survive.

00:05:37.116 --> 00:05:41.096
So, if you were to plant an orchid
in the same environment as a cactus

00:05:41.096 --> 00:05:43.467
and expect them both to bloom,

00:05:43.467 --> 00:05:45.987
you'll be sorely disappointed.

00:05:45.987 --> 00:05:49.249
Likewise, if you plant students
in the same classroom environment,

00:05:49.249 --> 00:05:51.456
and expect them all to flourish,

00:05:51.456 --> 00:05:54.487
you'll once again be disappointed.

00:05:54.487 --> 00:05:57.898
Not taking the time to think about
every student as an individual

00:05:57.898 --> 00:06:01.857
is like telling a flower 
that it's not beautiful

00:06:01.857 --> 00:06:04.287
and that it doesn't deserve to bloom

00:06:04.287 --> 00:06:09.628
because its petals aren't the exact same
shape or color as all the other flowers.

00:06:09.628 --> 00:06:11.708
But really, if you think about it,

00:06:11.708 --> 00:06:15.409
no two children or flowers 
are exactly alike.

00:06:15.409 --> 00:06:20.049
Imagine, instead, if schools were focused
on equity rather than on equality,

00:06:20.049 --> 00:06:22.219
on providing the students with
what they need

00:06:22.219 --> 00:06:25.519
rather than teaching everybody 
the same way.

00:06:25.519 --> 00:06:29.165
I strongly believe that the learning
environment should reflect

00:06:29.165 --> 00:06:33.348
the learning diversity among all students
in all schools.

00:06:33.348 --> 00:06:36.528
At my school, I'm in a class where we
have the opportunity to learn

00:06:36.528 --> 00:06:39.990
in whatever way best helps us
to understand.

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:42.191
If you could see us on a normal day,

00:06:42.191 --> 00:06:44.190
you might see some students standing,

00:06:44.190 --> 00:06:48.148
some sitting alone at a computer
with headphones in,

00:06:48.148 --> 00:06:49.860
some working in a small group,

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:54.019
and maybe even some working
with the teacher who is helping them.

00:06:54.019 --> 00:06:56.959
Now, to create this situation 
in every classroom

00:06:56.959 --> 00:06:59.380
wouldn't be easy at all.

00:06:59.380 --> 00:07:03.200
It would take extra time,
extra effort, and extra manpower,

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:04.118
but in the end,

00:07:04.118 --> 00:07:07.600
leveling the playing field would instill 
self-confidence in the students

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:09.860
and help them to achieve 
their full potential

00:07:09.860 --> 00:07:14.221
to shine bigger and brighter
than ever.

00:07:14.221 --> 00:07:18.857
My message for each and every one
of you today

00:07:18.857 --> 00:07:23.501
is not to overlook those two
seemingly insignificant letters

00:07:23.501 --> 00:07:25.699
because though they are small,

00:07:25.699 --> 00:07:30.041
they represent the difference
between equality and equity,

00:07:30.041 --> 00:07:33.070
between fairness and sameness.

00:07:33.070 --> 00:07:38.723
And my question is what flower
doesn't deserve to bloom?

00:07:38.723 --> 00:07:39.972
Thank you.

